  <@step id="memory-recall" title="Memory recall" phrase="Sidecar recalls memory into later work"
    group="learn">
    <@what>
      A cheap read-only sidecar model (the kibitzer) wakes only when a new recall candidate appears, at most 2 charged wakes per 10 minutes, with 8 tool calls and 90 seconds per wake. Its tool list (read, grep, session entries, memory lookup, nudge) is fixed in code, so it cannot write. It delivers at most 5 hints (default 2) of 200 characters as a hidden steering message; it cannot stop a turn or block a tool call. Memory is a git repo. Code journals every settled turn and extracts facts after 4 settled turns; reflection fires on agent end, settle and compaction; a consolidation pass runs after 30 idle minutes, at most once in 24 hours. Recall candidates are collected at prompt and tool-call hooks and injected before the next turn as a hidden message. Nothing ties memory to a plan, a commit or a PR. No retrospective step.
    </@what>
    <@starts>
      Nothing in the plan lane starts it: these listeners fire on session events in every session.
